{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1175968-has-anyone-converted-salvage-worlds-sytem-to-the-cod-storyteling-before","id":1175968,"title":"Has anyone converted Salvage Worlds sytem to the CoD Storyteling before?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-01-14T07:04:01-08:00","id":1175968,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Has anyone converted Salvage Worlds sytem to the CoD Storyteling before?","content":"I'm preparing a crossover game play between Deadlands; Reloaded (SW) and CoD cosmologies, based mostly in the CoD rule system while trying to keep or even adding up own Poker Hands system for some game situations (yep, the idea is to play some quick poker hands as well, maybe for Combat? Definitely for Magic, Gambling...).<br /> <br /> Conversion doesn't sounds too hard since SW traits count up as 5 &quot;levels&quot; (d4 ,d6, d8, d10 and d12) and there's the Edges that can easily become Merits, etc. Just wondering if anyone has ever written it down already because I come from the oWofD times and still have not played either Savage Worlds or nWofD (CoD) yet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23698-eien-hawkmoon","name":"Eien Hawkmoon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23698_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-15T04:40:52-08:00","id":1176248,"title":"","content":"I'm basing my <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/857534-dark-eras-battle-mass-combat-rules\">Mass Combat rules</a> as mix between Savage Worlds rules variant - that are executed in CoD systems. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/cool.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Cool\" smilieid=\"6\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-01-16T10:40:38-08:00","id":1176542,"title":"","content":"What aspects of Savage Worlds do you want to implement? I think very few things need to be implemented in CofD, as both systems are pretty complete in my opinion and do their own things. I use Savage Worlds for fast, action-packed games and CofD for slightly more down to earth games. If you want a Deadlands game in the CofD it's easy: set it during the wild west and ramp up on the supernatural elements and possibly go with the premise that humanity knows about the supernatural. I have not played Deadlands so I know little more than the most superficial things about it.<br /> <br /> I know from your Owod-thread here that you do not like power imbalances between splats, at least not how imbalanced mages are. In that case Hunter: Mortal Remains could help you to construct PCs with supernatural powers based on the Dread Powers in it, and maybe use Witchfinders to simulate the shamans and other magic-users in Deadlands.<br /> <br /> There are plenty of Combat Edges that surely can be converted to CofD, for example the entire tree of Leadership Edges that has little comparison in CofD. Before commiting to that work though, you could possibly have a look at Hurt Locker which offers a whole slew of new Merits, most of them combat-oriented.<br /> <br /> When it comes to supernatural abilities, Savage Worlds has a flexible system of powers that are rather basic but are given character and colour with trappings. So four characters can have the Bolt power which is mechanically identical for each four of them, but in the narrative one of them is a wizard throwing lightning bolts, one is a mad scientist with a raygun, another is a druid launching stinging bees from their mouth and a fourth is a superhero who fires laserbeams from their eyes. The Chronicles of Darkness core book as well as Hunter: Mortal Remains offer something a bit similar with Dread Powers though these are somewhat more horror-themed.<br /> <br /> Summary: So my recommendation is to just go with CofD until you learn it a bit better and start homebrewing after that, but if you wish to start homebrewing right now then I'd say the Edges in Savage Worlds would be the most interesting parts to pilfer from, and possibly also the system of mechanically generic powers that are given narrative differentiation through trappings.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9849-aristarkos","name":"Aristarkos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9849_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"369"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}